            # global -- measured, all three), or INTERROGATED anywhere
            # (measured: `for p in [7,8]` with a post-loop `prev of p` leaves
            # p at the incoming value while the tape holds 7,8).
            #
            # The first version REFUSED this configuration -- and evicted DMG
            # from the envelope: `i is 1` in its argv parsing collides with
            # `for i in range of 23040` inside a render function, so the
            # flagship consumer (5.70 MHz, 136% of real hardware) stopped
            # AOT-compiling, visible only as one census row. Loop-scoping is
            # EMITTABLE, not just refusable: DECLARING the same C name inside
            # the loop body makes C's own block scoping do exactly the VM's
            # chain walk (the #109 numeric-shadow move). Body reads hit the
            # block-local, post-loop reads hit the outer binding, and the
            # binder store still traces (tape writes record in every scope).
            #
            # NUMERIC only. A boxed binder would need per-iteration decrefs
            # threaded past `break`, and an OBSERVED program's body reads
            # resolve through the env, which a C block-local cannot shadow --
            # both refuse rather than answer wrongly.
            if g_observed == 1:
                throw of ("AOT: '" + fvar + "' is a loop-scoped for binder (module-bound or interrogated name) in an OBSERVED program; observed reads resolve through the env, which a block-scoped C variable cannot shadow (see EigenScript#1056).")
            if bt[fvar] == 1:
                throw of ("AOT: '" + fvar + "' is a loop-scoped for binder over a BOXED binding; per-iteration ownership past break is not emitted yet (see EigenScript#1056).")
            # (round 120, #198) the block-local is a C double, so the
            # iterable must PROVABLY yield numbers (range, a buffer, an
            # all-numeric literal list -- the unbox scan's own test). A
            # string binder built and died at run time with the AOT's own
            # message where the VM prints a null b a; refuse by name at
            # build time instead. A boxed loop-scoped binder is the
            # unbuilt arm above.
